# Phase T: cooperative threads. The network has a thread of its own, so the OS
# keeps answering while a command waits for something else.
# The OS is 10.0.0.2 (52:54:00:be:e0:01); the test plays 10.0.0.3 (02:00:00:00:00:0b).
@wire
@ai-offline
$ threads
~ ^ ID  NAME       STATE         RUNS  STACK USED$
~ ^  0  main       running +\d+  \(boot stack\)$
~ ^ +\d+  net        waiting +\d+  \d+ of 256 KiB$
$ net static 10.0.0.2/24
@frame ^ffffffffffff525400bee00108060001080006040001525400bee0010a0000020000000000000a000002
@send ffffffffffff02000000000b0806000108000604000102000000000b0a0000030000000000000a000002000000000000000000000000000000000000
@frame ^02000000000b525400bee00108060001080006040002
# `ask` waits 3 seconds for an AI that is not there. A ping sent meanwhile is
# answered within a second, not after the command has finished.
$& ask hello
@send 525400bee00102000000000b08004500002400014000400126d40a0000030a00000208005b580b0b0007616263646566676800000000000000000000
@frame-soon ^02000000000b525400bee001080045000024[0-9a-f]{4}40004001[0-9a-f]{4}0a0000020a000003000063580b0b00076162636465666768
$wait
AI offline: beehive is not running on the host (start it with: make beehive)
# The self-tests start and end threads of their own, including a second shell;
# afterwards only the two permanent threads are left.
$ selftest
~ ^  ok    threads: take turns, sleep, end$
~ ^  ok    threads: no switch while the file system is in use$
~ ^  ok    threads: a second shell runs in its own session$
~ ^selftest: \d+ passed, 0 failed$
$ threads
~ main       running
~ net        waiting
!~ shell2
!~ ready
